WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.359
Today’s protagonist was born as a Prince
in a southern Indian kingdom, but he soon

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:09.190
discarded his palace life to embrace the study
of Buddhism.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:14.540
As a travelling Master, he journeyed to China,
where he gained powerful disciples and founded

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.869
Dhyana Buddhism, better known in China as
Chan, and later in Japan as Zen.

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:24.880
He is also credited as the first Kung Fu Master
at the Temple of Shaolin.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.859
He popularised meditation, introduced green
tea to China, and had a curious second life

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:31.699
as a popular Japanese doll.

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:36.610
Put the kettle on the stove, control your
breathing, and sink into the life and legend

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:41.870
of Bodhidharma, known as Da Mo in China and
Daruma in Japan.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:58.690
Here is the life and legacy of the 28th Buddha:
The Prince of Pallava

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:03.030
Most of what we know about Bodhidharma is
painted in the colours of legend; however,

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:08.210
there is a historical account of his life
written by the Chinese monk Daoxuan in the

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.710
7th Century AD.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.060
Bodhidharma’s exact date of birth is not
known.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.979
We do know that he was active throughout the
first half of the 6th Century AD and was born

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:24.159
in the South Indian Kingdom of Pallava, corresponding
to the present-day States of Tamil Nadu, Karnataka,

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:25.420
and Andhra Telangana.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:30.979
At birth he was called Bodhyottara, and he
wasn’t exactly just another citizen of Pallava

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:36.450
-- he was a Royal Prince, the third and youngest
son of King Simhavarman II.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:41.319
The capital city of Pallava was a placed called
Kanchi, and it was a prominent centre of Buddhist

00:01:41.319 --> 00:01:45.340
scholarship, giving birth to many masters
of the Mahayana strand of Buddhism.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:51.420
The King himself was interested in the Dharma,
a term which indicates the words of the Buddha,

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.299
the practice of his teaching, and the attainment
of enlightenment.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:02:01.450
That is why he arranged for he and his sons
to be trained by the famed master Prajnottara.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:06.460
Prajnottara was the 27th in a lineage of masters
and disciples that could be traced back directly

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:12.280
to Siddhartha Gautama, known simply as the
Buddha, the famous founder of the religion.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.430
During the course of the training, the master
noticed the spiritual potential and wisdom

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:21.510
of the youngest prince, but he decided to
wait before making of Bodhyottara his chosen

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:22.510
disciple.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.390
The time was finally ripe when the King died.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:29.099
While everyone was in mourning, Bodhyottara
remained in a state of deep, constant meditation

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:30.849
for seven consecutive days.

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:36.700
After finally rising a full week later, he
went to Prajnottara and requested to be accepted

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.959
as his disciple.

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:42.900
Prajnottara took him under his wing and imparted
all the necessary teachings to make of him

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.120
a new master.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.459
Under the guidance of his mentor, Bodhyottara
deepened his understanding of the Dharma,

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:52.490
but also mastered Ayurvedic medicine, acupressure
and martial arts.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:58.090
When he eventually became bodhi or ‘awakened’,
Prajnottara gave him his new name: Bodhidharma.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.920
He had now become the 28th in that unbroken
lineage of realized Masters.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:09.862
Buddhism in (about) 3 minutes
I expect that by now your water has boiled.

00:03:09.862 --> 00:03:14.560
As your green tea brews for the three minutes
required for the perfect cup, let me give

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.349
you a quick summary of the main tenets of
Buddhism.

00:03:17.349 --> 00:03:22.651
Buddhism is a religion based on the teachings
of Siddhartha Gautama, born in the 5th century

00:03:22.651 --> 00:03:25.010
BC in modern-day Nepal.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:30.250
He was known by his title "the Buddha," meaning
"the awakened one," after he experienced a

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:35.500
profound realization about the nature of life,
death, and existence.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.769
The Buddha travelled and taught throughout
his life, but he didn't teach people what

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:42.709
he had begun to understand when he became
awakened.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:47.720
Instead, he taught people how to realize this
awakening for themselves, which would arrive

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.980
through their own direct experience, rather
than beliefs and dogmas.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:57.000
He wasn’t offering an answer; he was teaching
his pupils how to ask the right question.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:02.249
By the 3rd century BC, Buddhism had become
state religion in India, and from there it

00:04:02.249 --> 00:04:03.739
spread throughout the rest of Asia.

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:08.519
Today, the number of Buddhists is estimated
to be about 350 million, which makes Buddhism

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:13.249
the fourth largest religion in the world … although
it is questioned whether it should be considered

00:04:13.249 --> 00:04:14.470
a religion at all.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:20.090
Buddhism is non-theistic at its core and coexists
well with other established religions.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:24.360
As a result, some scholars choose to consider
it as more of a philosophical doctrine than

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.630
an explicit religion.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:31.570
Nomenclature aside, the detail that sets Buddhism
apart from other religions is the lack of

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.810
dogmatic truths to be memorized and believed.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:40.810
The Buddha taught his followers how to realize
truth for yourself, so its focus is on practice,

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.130
rather than belief.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:47.080
In spite of this emphasis on free inquiry,
Buddhism does provide some explicit beliefs,

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:51.030
known as the Four Noble Truths:
The truth of suffering, or "dukkha"

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:56.440
The truth of the cause of suffering, or "samudaya"
The truth of the end of suffering, or "nirhodha"

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.840
The truth of the path that frees us from suffering,
or "magga"

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.260
Practitioners are encouraged to explore the
teachings that shape these truths and test

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:06.462
them against their own experience.

00:05:06.462 --> 00:05:09.210
And while these truths have been consistent
across time, about 2,000 years ago Buddhism

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:15.000
divided into two major schools: Theravada
and Mahayana, which I mentioned earlier, and

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.620
is the school to which Bodhidharma adhered
to.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.830
The two schools differ in their understanding
of the concept of "anatta."

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:27.200
Buddhists believe there is no "self" in the
sense of a permanent, integral, autonomous

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.370
being within an individual existence.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:34.430
The Theravada school considers anatta to mean
that an individual's ego or personality is

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:35.479
a delusion.

00:05:35.479 --> 00:05:40.759
Once freed of this delusion, the individual
can enjoy the blissful state of Nirvana.

00:05:40.759 --> 00:05:46.599
Mahayana Buddhism goes further, considering
all conscious phenomena as void of intrinsic

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:47.599
identity.

00:05:47.599 --> 00:05:50.440
They craft identity only in relation to other
phenomena.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:55.919
The implication is that there is neither reality
nor unreality, only relativity.

00:05:55.919 --> 00:05:59.139
This teaching is called "shunyata," or "emptiness."

00:05:59.139 --> 00:06:02.509
Another defining aspect of Mahayana is the
‘Buddha Nature’.

00:06:02.509 --> 00:06:07.050
This is a fundamental nature accessible to
all beings, which offers the potential to

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.800
reach the awakening, or enlightenment, through
practice.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.819
According to 13th Century Japanese Monk Eihei
Dogen, enlightenment is intrinsic to the Buddha

00:06:16.819 --> 00:06:17.819
nature.

00:06:17.819 --> 00:06:22.169
Therefore, all beings are already enlightened,
but we need to clarify the nature of our existence

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:25.789
to experience the awakening that was always
there.

00:06:25.789 --> 00:06:32.170
And with that, it’s time to sip your tea,
while I continue with our story.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:38.879
The Emperor and the General
After completing his training, Bodhidharma

00:06:38.879 --> 00:06:44.710
was instructed by Prajnottara to travel to
China and impart the teachings of Mahayana.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:49.770
Upon arriving, Bodhidharma felt that people
were too focused on the conventions of Buddhism,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.099
rather than its essence.

00:06:51.099 --> 00:06:55.979
They were too preoccupied with building temples
and chanting, rather than looking into their

00:06:55.979 --> 00:06:58.319
own minds for spiritual growth.

00:06:58.319 --> 00:07:02.780
Some time after arriving, Bodhidharma was
invited to a monastery to deliver a lesson

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:07.909
to a large number of students, but the Master
just sat in front of them, meditating.

00:07:07.909 --> 00:07:13.500
Hours passed by, with Bodhidharma ever undisturbed
by the whispers and murmurs of an impatient

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.500
assembly.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.530
Eventually, he just silently stood up and
walked away.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:18.909
Had he gone crazy?

00:07:18.909 --> 00:07:21.949
Was there a mystical meaning behind his silence?

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:26.629
His simple intent was to shock them, to get
students to consider abandoning the pursuit

00:07:26.629 --> 00:07:28.819
of the exterior trappings of Buddhism.

00:07:28.819 --> 00:07:34.030
To focus on a journey toward the realization
of ‘shunyata’, emptiness, through the

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:39.520
means of meditation: Dhyana in Sanskrit, or
Chan in Chinese.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.180
The news of these teaching methods reached
Emperor Wu of Southern China, an avid practitioner

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:45.229
of Mahayana Buddhism.

00:07:45.229 --> 00:07:50.150
He supported the monastic orders generously,
built many Temples, promoted vegetarianism

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:53.300
and banned the sacrifice of animals and capital
punishment.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.449
Believing he had earned an audience with a
wise master like Bodhidharma, Wu summoned

00:07:57.449 --> 00:08:01.300
the Indian master to his court and recounted
his own great deeds.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.610
He then asked,
“Having done all these, how much merit do

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:05.650
I have?”.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:08.199
Bodhidharma replied:
“None”.

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:10.879
The Emperor was taken aback by this answer
and pressed on:

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:13.810
“What is the first principle of the holy
teachings?”

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:17.199
Bodhidharma replied:
“Vast emptiness, nothing holy.”

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:20.689
Bewildered, the Emperor continued:
“Who is standing before me then?”

00:08:20.689 --> 00:08:23.220
Bodhidharma replied:
“I don’t know”.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:27.029
This conversation may have never happened,
but it is one of the first recorded Koans

00:08:27.029 --> 00:08:32.339
-- paradoxical anecdotes or riddles, used
in Zen Buddhism to invite deep introspection.

00:08:32.339 --> 00:08:37.160
The meaning of this koan is to undermine the
idea of identity and holiness.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:41.400
The Emperor is portrayed as performing good
deeds, but these are just meant to reinforce

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.700
his ego, to use a modern concept.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:49.330
A king who clings to his ego will never find
the bliss of Nirvana, or the freedom of liberation.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:53.480
For the second question of the Emperor, the
Master’s response points out that there

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:56.170
is nothing to cling to as ‘holy’.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:58.180
Everything is inherently holy.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:03.420
Trying to differentiate between ‘holy’
and ‘unholy’ is quintessentially unholy.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:08.010
And the third response shatters again the
concept of ego, this time for Bodhidharma

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:09.010
himself.

00:09:09.010 --> 00:09:13.360
As his thoughts are passing through him like
water in a river, nothing stays as the true

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:14.510
Bodhidharma.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.290
Self-image is only a mirage.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:22.470
The story goes that Wu did not fully comprehend
Bodhidharma’s teachings, and continued on

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.760
without a true sense of understanding.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:29.760
Only later did he realise the depth of Bodhidharma’s
message, and he lamented:

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:30.760
‘Alas!

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:35.850
I saw him without seeing him; I met him without
meeting him; I encountered him without encountering

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:39.610
him; Now, as before, I regret this deeply!’

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:42.871
Bodhidharma continued his journey to the city
of Nanjing, on the Yangtse River.

00:09:42.871 --> 00:09:47.660
There, he came across a large crowd rallied
around a famous Buddhist monk, Shen Guang,

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:53.050
who happened to be a former warrior, a General,
and one who had not entirely lost his taste

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:54.120
for aggression.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.730
Bodhidharma sat down to listen to Shen Guang,
but his constant nodding irritated the ex-soldier.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:04.130
Eventually, Shen Guang raised his rosary of
heavy beads and hit Bodhidharma in the face,

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:05.180
out of frustration.

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:10.079
The blow knocked out two of Bodhidharma’s
teeth … but the Master just smiled and walked

00:10:10.079 --> 00:10:11.079
away.

00:10:11.079 --> 00:10:15.160
Shen Guang was intrigued by this reaction
and immediately started following the Indian

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:16.160
master.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:19.829
When he reached the banks of the Yangtse River,
he was startled by the sight of Bodhidharma

00:10:19.829 --> 00:10:24.350
gently gliding through the water, standing
on a single reed, as if completely weightless.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.610
Well, he could do that, too, couldn’t he?

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:30.680
Seeing an old lady sitting with a bundle of
reeds on the bank, Shen Guang snatched two

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:34.899
reeds off her, tossed them on the river and
jumped on them.

00:10:34.899 --> 00:10:35.920
Naturally, he drowned.

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:39.079
Well, almost, as the old lady kind enough
to pull him out of the water.

00:10:39.079 --> 00:10:43.329
As Shen Guang’s anger boiled over, he realised
that the man he was following may have been

00:10:43.329 --> 00:10:45.589
trying to teach him a lesson, literally.

00:10:45.589 --> 00:10:50.620
He had acted out of aggression and mindlessness,
without considering for a second that Bodhidharma

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:55.380
may have been able to sail on a single reed
by virtue of his mindfulness.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:59.490
Shen Guang had been dragged down into the
water by the weight of his physical body,

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.820
as well as the weight of his own self.

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:06.839
The former General had run after Bodhidharma
out of curiosity, but now all he wanted was

00:11:06.839 --> 00:11:08.070
to really know the man.

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:11.360
He wished to become his disciple.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:18.350
The Masters of Shaolin
Bodhidharma continued his journey, and around

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:25.250
the year 527 AD he finally reached his destination:
The Temple of Shaolin, one of the major centres

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:31.519
of Buddhism in China, founded by another Indian
master, Buddhab hadra, in 495 AD.

00:11:31.519 --> 00:11:35.380
The Shaolin monks had heard of his approach
and were gathered to meet him.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:39.410
When they invited Bodhidharma to come stay
at the temple, though, he did not reply.

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:45.220
Instead, he walked to a nearby cave on a mountain
behind Shaolin, sat down, and began meditating.

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:50.180
Here, the stories tell us that the Indian
master sat facing a cave wall and meditated

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.100
for nine consecutive years.

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:56.389
According to one story, his concentration
was so intense that the image of his body

00:11:56.389 --> 00:11:59.690
was engraved into the stone of the wall before
him.

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:04.040
According to another account, his stillness
was so unbroken that all four limbs eventually

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:05.880
withered and fell off.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:10.180
Other legends claim that Bodhidharma was unable
to stay fully awake during his nine years

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:11.180
of meditation.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:14.350
Out of frustration, he pulled out a knife
and proceeded to cut off his eyelids.

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:19.110
As the two scraps of skin fell on the ground,
a tea leaf sprouted on the very spot, the

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:20.839
first to ever grow in China.

00:12:20.839 --> 00:12:25.240
But for now, I am going to stick to an account
in which the Master’s body remained intact

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:26.360
inside the cave.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:28.550
In the meanwhile, Shen Guang had caught up
with him.

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:33.449
Unable to get a word out of Bodhidharma, the
Chinese monk decided to stay outside the cave

00:12:33.449 --> 00:12:34.639
and stand guard.

00:12:34.639 --> 00:12:39.610
Every so often, both Shen Guang and the Shaolin
monks would try and interact with Bodhidharma,

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:42.010
but no one ever received a reaction.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:46.050
Finally, after nine years had passed, the
puzzled monks decided that they had to do

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:47.850
something for the enigmatic Master.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.649
So, they built a special room inside the Temple,
just for him.

00:12:51.649 --> 00:12:56.040
The Shaolin monks invited Bodhidharma to come
and stay in the room, to which he did not

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:57.459
immediately respond.

00:12:57.459 --> 00:13:03.230
Instead, he stood up, walked down to the room,
sat down, and immediately began meditating.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:04.639
For another four years!

00:13:04.639 --> 00:13:08.290
Shen Guang had been standing guard now for
a total of 13 winters.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:11.260
All his requests to be taught by Bodhidharma
had been ignored.

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:15.889
In a fit of frustration, Shen Guang picked
a large block of snow and ice and hurled it

00:13:15.889 --> 00:13:17.040
into the Master’s room.

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:21.610
This massive snowball had the intended effect
of awaking Bodhidharma from his meditation.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:24.880
Shen Guang demanded to know, once again, when
he would teach him.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:26.170
The reply he received?

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:31.260
‘When red snow will fall from the sky’
Something inside Shen Guang snapped and he

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.990
knew immediately what to do.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:35.639
As a former warrior, he always carried a sword
with him.

00:13:35.639 --> 00:13:40.029
He raised it high above his head and swung
it downwards in a fast, powerful, cutting

00:13:40.029 --> 00:13:41.029
motion.

00:13:41.029 --> 00:13:42.930
He had just cut off his own left arm.

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:47.029
Shen Guang grabbed his severed limb, raised
it above his head and whirled it around.

00:13:47.029 --> 00:13:51.410
The blood still gushing from the left arm
froze in mid-air and floated to the ground

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:52.760
like red snow.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:56.110
Seeing this act of self-sacrifice, Bodhidharma
agreed to teach Shen Guang.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:59.660
Their subsequent conversation became another
founding Koan of Zen.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:02.570
Shen Guang beseeched the Master:
“My mind has no peace yet!

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:05.839
I beg you, O Master, please put it to rest!”

00:14:05.839 --> 00:14:09.120
Bodhidharma said
“Bring your mind here and I will pacify

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:11.770
it”
Shen Guang searched for a while, then responded:

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:15.380
“I have searched for my mind, and I cannot
take hold of it.”

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:18.800
Then Bodhidharma declared, “Now your mind
is pacified.”

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:22.800
The next lesson from Bodhidharma would require
four more years of Shen Guang’s life.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:27.690
Over that span, Bodhidharma asked him to live
on top of Drum Mountain, in front of the Shaolin

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:28.690
Temple.

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:32.190
At the beginning of each year, the Master
would dig up a well, to provide for all of

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:33.589
Shen Guang’s needs.

00:14:33.589 --> 00:14:37.829
Each year, the water from the Master’s wells
had a different taste: first bitter, then

00:14:37.829 --> 00:14:40.540
spicy, next: sour and finally: sweet.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:44.490
At this point, Shen Guang realized that the
four wells represented his life.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:48.940
Like the wells, his life would sometimes be
bitter, sometimes sour, sometimes spicy and

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:50.060
sometimes sweet.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:54.579
Each of these phases in his life was equally
beautiful and necessary, just as each of the

00:14:54.579 --> 00:14:58.500
four seasons of the year is beautiful and
necessary in its own way.

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:02.790
Bodhidharma had imparted this lesson almost
without using any words, a type of “action

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:06.949
language” that is fundamental to Chan, or
Zen, Buddhism.

00:15:06.949 --> 00:15:12.449
After this final ordeal and realization, Shen
Guang was given the name Hui Ke by his master,

00:15:12.449 --> 00:15:15.680
and he became the abbot of Shaolin after Bodhidharma.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:19.720
To pay respect for the sacrifice which Hui
Ke made, disciples and monks of the Shaolin

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:22.580
Temple greet each other using only their right
hand.

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:31.130
Zen and the Martial Arts
As I mentioned earlier, most of Bodhidharma’s

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.630
life has been mystified by legend.

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:37.680
But assuming what you heard is all fact, you
may be wondering: While Shen Guang was barely

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:42.209
surviving on water at the top of Drum Mountain,
what was Bodhidharma up to?

00:15:42.209 --> 00:15:45.761
We can assume it was during this four-year
period that he shared his teachings with the

00:15:45.761 --> 00:15:46.920
Shaolin monks.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:50.060
According to some traditional accounts, he
may have taught them how to fight.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:54.090
The Chinese martial arts, collectively known
in the West as Kung Fu, may have been first

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:58.040
introduced in the Country by an Indian wandering
monk.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:15:59.040
Could this be true?

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.430
Well, as a Royal Prince, the young Bodhidharma
may have been already skilled the traditional

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:05.040
Indian martial arts, such as Kalaripaiattu.

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:08.960
Moreover, he received additional training
from his Buddhist master Prajnottara.

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:12.990
And some artwork at the Shaolin depicts a
dark-skinned monk training lighter skinned

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.649
students in a variety of hand-to-hand techniques.

00:16:15.649 --> 00:16:19.779
However, the predominant point of view among
current Zen scholars is that the claim that

00:16:19.779 --> 00:16:22.839
Bodhidharma invented Kung Fu is wildly exaggerated.

00:16:22.839 --> 00:16:27.310
For one thing, the first codified Chinese
martial art, Jiao-di, pre-dates the Indian

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:29.680
master by at least 30 centuries.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:34.570
And while there is a record of Bodhidharma
teaching some physical exercises to the monks,

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:36.949
these are not necessarily related to combat.

00:16:36.949 --> 00:16:41.500
The exercises known as the “18 hands of
Lohan” were intended to build up the monks’

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:46.589
Qi, or vital energy, to improve their general
health and nourish their brains – a necessary

00:16:46.589 --> 00:16:48.589
step to attain awakening.

00:16:48.589 --> 00:16:52.769
Some scholars may argue that a more realistic
interpretation of Bodhidharma’s time at

00:16:52.769 --> 00:16:57.500
Shaolin is that he perfected and taught his
new branch of Buddhism, Chan or Zen.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:02.540
Now I guess it’s time for me to explain
what Zen Buddhism is about.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:07.470
Bodhidharma himself defined Zen as:
"A special transmission outside the scriptures;

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:12.680
No dependence on words and letters;
Direct pointing to the mind of man;

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:14.840
Seeing into one's nature and attaining Buddhahood."

00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:20.030
In other words, Zen requires a face-to-face
transmission of Buddhist precepts, from teacher

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:22.170
to student, outside the written tradition.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:25.350
And to do that, you need a proper certified
Zen teacher, which I am not.

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:30.580
I can only add that Zen is not an intellectual
discipline you can learn from books, or a

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:31.580
video.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:34.700
Instead, it's a practice of studying the mind
and seeing into one's nature.

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:38.980
According to Bodhidharma, the main tool of
this is the practice of meditation, best known

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:41.210
from its Japanese name: zazen.

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:45.930
Beginners approaching zazen are taught to
work with their breath to learn concentration.

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:48.680
After a few months, your ability to concentrate
may have ripened.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:51.950
As you progress, you may understand that the
true goal of zazen … is that there should

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:57.690
not be any goal, nor expectation to a particular
meditation session.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:01.750
Practitioners should learn to ‘just sit’
in meditation – shikantaza – while learning

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:06.650
a lot about themselves along the way.

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:11.760
Daruma
Bodhidharma’s lofty teaching in Shaolin

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:16.970
and China had won him a few disciples, but
also some powerful enemies, and we are told

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:19.750
that he was eventually poisoned by two rivals.

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:22.790
Allegedly, the sage survived both attempts.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:26.780
Eventually, during the third attempt, Bodhidharma
accepted to take the poison and died at the

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:29.120
reported age of 150.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:30.550
But was death the end?

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:35.340
Three years after his death, a Chinese diplomat
named Sòng Yún was returning to China from

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:38.410
a trip to the West when he met none other
than Bodhidharma.

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:42.930
He was on his way back to India, walking barefoot
and carrying one shoe in his hand.

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:47.470
When the diplomat finally got home, and told
this story, the master’s grave was opened.

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:50.660
Inside, it was empty, except for one shoe.

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:54.800
Another version of Bodhidharma’s last years
on Earth tells of how the master left China

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:59.900
for Japan, where he, disguised as a beggar,
met with Prince Shōtoku Taishi, celebrated

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.660
as the first great patron of Buddhism in Japan.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:07.540
While Bodhidharma’s travels to Japan are
strongly disputed, it is a fact that his teachings

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:09.070
did gain great popularity there.

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:13.742
The master became known as Daruma, and his
appearance and story inspired one of the most

00:19:13.742 --> 00:19:19.300
popular symbols of Japanese folklore, the
Daruma doll, popular with locals and tourists

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:20.300
alike.

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:24.110
If you have visited Japan, you may have taken
home one of these, wondering what’s the

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:27.770
reason behind the round shape and the blank,
white eyes.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:31.600
The shape of these dolls reflects the nine
years that Daruma spent meditating in the

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.090
cave near Shaolin.

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:34.090
Remember?

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:36.510
His immobility was such that all his limbs
had fallen off.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:40.890
The wide-opened eyes of the doll remind us
of how the master had cut off his eyelids

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:42.690
to remain awake during zazen.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:46.370
The dolls are normally made without their
pupils, it is up to their owners to draw them

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:47.630
once their wishes are fulfilled.

00:19:47.630 --> 00:19:51.800
Over time, the doll took a life of its own,
collecting attributes further and further

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:54.090
removed from the original inspiration.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:58.010
For example, it was dressed in red, a colour
associated with measles and smallpox, and

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:01.110
became a talisman to protect children against
these diseases.

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:06.610
In a surprising twist, artists of the Edo
era (1603 to 1868) even morphed these dolls

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:07.870
into phallic symbols.

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:11.680
The sexual attributes were then shifted onto
the sage himself, who became the object of

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:15.980
parody-filled illustrations in which he leers
at prostitutes or courtesans.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:18.570
What a strange, worldly tribute to the man
who once said

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:23.180
“To have a body is to suffer”
I hope you learned something new from today’s

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:26.800
video … and I hope that what you learned
is that you can’t really learn anything

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:39.300
by reading a book or watching 
a video.

